 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 3,
   "plaque": "hebrides_gaelic_roads",
   "title": "Roads in Two Languages",
   "text": "Road signs in the Outer Hebrides are in Gaelic first and English second, and a good few are in Gaelic only. Stornoway is Ste\u00f2rnabhagh, Harris is Na Hearadh and Lewis is Le\u00f2dhas. Learn two words, ceud m\u00ecle f\u00e0ilte, a hundred thousand welcomes, and you are halfway there."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "hebrides_gearrannan",
   "title": "Gearrannan",
   "text": "Gearrannan is a village of blackhouses (taighean dubha) on the Atlantic cliffs of Lewis, where families lived until 1974. They were saved, thatched afresh and now take visitors. Ropes weighted with stones hold the roofs down against the gales."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "hebrides_blackhouse",
   "title": "The Blackhouse",
   "text": "A taigh dubh has thick double drystone walls with an earth core, a thatched roof and a peat fire in the middle of the floor with no chimney. The smoke seeped up through the thatch, and the old thatch, soaked with soot, was spread on the fields as manure."
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 9,
   "plaque": "hebrides_callanish",
   "title": "Callanish",
   "text": "Calanais is a cross-shaped setting of standing stones, raised about 5,000 years ago, older than Stonehenge. Thirteen stones form a ring around a tall central stone nearly five metres high, with an avenue of stones running north and rows to the east, south and west."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 16,
   "plaque": "hebrides_callanish_moon",
   "title": "The Moon and the Sleeping Woman",
   "text": "Every 18.6 years the moon skims very low over the southern hills here, and the long line of hills is said to look like a sleeping woman, Cailleach na Mointich, the old woman of the moors. Some think the stones were laid out to watch it. Legend says they were giants turned to stone for refusing to be baptised."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "hebrides_uig_sands",
   "title": "Uig Sands",
   "text": "In 1831 a hoard of 93 carved walrus-ivory chess pieces turned up in a sandbank at Uig, on the west of Lewis. Tradition says a local man dug them out of a dune. They were probably carved in Norway about 800 years ago, and most are now in the British Museum and the National Museum of Scotland."
  },
  {
   "x": 17,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "hebrides_berserkers",
   "title": "The Berserkers",
   "text": "The rooks among the Lewis Chessmen are carved as berserkers, Norse warriors in a battle frenzy, biting the tops of their shields. The kings, queens, bishops and knights are all solemn and a little worried. The berserkers are having the best time on the board."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 44,
   "plaque": "hebrides_luskentyre",
   "title": "Luskentyre",
   "text": "Tr\u00e0igh Losgaintir has white sand made of crushed shell, and shallow water that turns turquoise in the sun, which makes it look like somewhere much warmer than the Atlantic. It is often named among the loveliest beaches in the world. The water is still the Atlantic."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 31,
   "plaque": "hebrides_machair",
   "title": "The Machair",
   "text": "Machair (a Gaelic word) is a low, fertile grassland on the Atlantic edge of Scotland and Ireland, fed by sea-shell sand blown inland. In June and July it is a meadow of buttercups, clover, orchids and lady's bedstraw. It is among the rarest habitats in Europe, and the islands keep it by crofting."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "hebrides_corncrake",
   "title": "The Corncrake",
   "text": "The corncrake's call is a rasping crex crex that goes on all night. The bird flies from Africa each spring and hides in tall meadow grass, so you hear far more than you see. The Hebrides are one of its last strongholds, because crofters mow late and in ways that leave the chicks a way out."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "hebrides_harris_tweed",
   "title": "Harris Tweed",
   "text": "By law Harris Tweed must be handwoven by islanders at their own homes in the Outer Hebrides, from pure virgin wool dyed and spun on the islands. The Orb mark, a globe with a cross, has stood for that since 1909 and is stamped on every length. The Harris Tweed Act of 1993 put the rule into law."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 51,
   "plaque": "hebrides_tweed_dye",
   "title": "Crotal and the Colours",
   "text": "Island dyers once made their colours from what was growing round them: heather, yellow iris and crotal, a grey lichen scraped from rocks that dyes wool a rusty orange-brown. Crotal is why old Harris Tweed smells faintly of the hill after rain."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 44,
   "plaque": "hebrides_peat",
   "title": "Peat",
   "text": "Peat (m\u00f2ine) is old bog, centuries of plants pressed together, cut in spring with a tairsgeir, a special peat spade, then stacked in rickles to dry on the wind. It has heated island homes for centuries, and it still flavours some island malt whisky. Each family has its own peat bank."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "hebrides_clisham",
   "title": "An Cliseam",
   "text": "An Cliseam, the Clisham, is the highest hill in the Western Isles at 799 metres, in the North Harris hills. The road north from Tarbert climbs past its shoulders, and on a clear day you can look from the top across to Skye."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 57,
   "plaque": "hebrides_tarbert",
   "title": "Tairbeart",
   "text": "Tarbert's name comes from the Gaelic for an isthmus, because the village sits on a narrow neck of land between East and West Loch Tarbert, where North Harris meets South Harris. The ferry from Skye lands at the quay a few steps east of here."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 5,
   "text": "NORTH: Calanais (Callanish), Gearrannan and Ste\u00f2rnabhagh (Stornoway). SOUTH: Tairbeart (Tarbert) and the ferry to Skye."
  },
  {
   "x": 55,
   "y": 53,
   "text": "TAIRBEART. The ferry for Skye leaves from the quay here. Mind the gulls, they know when you have a sandwich."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 58,
   "text": "STORNOWAY, 36 miles north by the road you're standing on. The sheep have right of way."
  }
 ],
